If (\ref{lp-semi-k}) is exact at level $d\in\mathbb{N}$, then
\[L_d(\mathbf{x},\lambda^*)-f^*=\sigma(\mathbf{x}),\qquad\forall \mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^n,\]
for some $\lambda^*\geq0$ and some $\sigma\in\Sigma[\mathbf{x}]_k$. Equivalently,
\begin{eqnarray*}
f(\mathbf{x})-f^*\,=\,\sigma(\mathbf{x})&+&\sum_{(\alpha,\beta)\in\mathbb{N}^{2m}_{d}}\lambda^*_{\alpha\beta}\left(\prod_{j=1}^mg_j(\mathbf{x})^{\alpha_j}\right)\times\\
&&\left(\prod_{j=1}^m(1-g_j(\mathbf{x}))^{\beta_j}\right).\end{eqnarray*}
Then evaluating at $\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x}^*$ yields $\sigma(\mathbf{x}^*)=0$ and 
\[\lambda^*_{\alpha\beta}\,>0\,\Rightarrow\quad\left\{\begin{array}{l}
\exists j\in I_1(\mathbf{x}^*)\mbox{ s.t. }\alpha_j>0,\:\mbox{or}\\
\exists j\in I_2(\mathbf{x}^*)\mbox{ s.t. }\beta_j>0.\end{array}\right.\]
So let $\Omega\,:=\,\{(\alpha,\beta)\in\mathbb{N}^{2m}_{d}\::\:\lambda^*_{\alpha\beta}>0\}$ and for every
$(\alpha,\beta)\in\Omega$ let
\begin{eqnarray*}
J^1_{\alpha\beta}&:=&\,\{j\in I_1(\mathbf{x}^*)\,:\,\alpha_j>0\},\\
J^2_{\alpha\beta}&:=&\{j\in I_2(\mathbf{x}^*)\,:\,\beta_j>0\}.\end{eqnarray*}
Next, define $\mathbf{V}\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ to be the real variety:
\begin{eqnarray}
\nonumber

\left\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^n\right.&:&
\left(\prod_{j\in J^1_{\alpha\beta}}g_j(\mathbf{x})\right)
\left(\prod_{j\in J^2_{\alpha\beta}}(1-g_j(\mathbf{x}))\right)\,=\,0,\\
\label{real-variety}
&&\left.\forall\:(\alpha,\beta)\in\Omega\:\right\}.\end{eqnarray}
Then for every $\mathbf{x}\in\mathbf{V}$, one obtains $f(\mathbf{x})-f^*=\sigma(\mathbf{x})\geq0$, which means that
$f^*$ is the global minimum of $f$ on $\mathbf{V}$. If $k=0$ then $\sigma$ is constant and equal to zero.
And so $f(\mathbf{x})-f^*=0$ for all $\mathbf{x}\in\mathbf{V}$.
$\Box$

